          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p1\",\"quote\":\"Historically, the astronomical unit was conceived as the average Earth-Sun distance (the average of Earth's aphelion and perihelion), before its modern redefinition in 2012.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p11\",\"quote\":\"In 2012, finding that the equalization of relativity alone would make the definition overly complex, the IAU simply used the 2009 estimate to redefine the astronomical unit as a conventional unit of length directly tied to the metre (exactly 149 597 870 700 m).\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p14\",\"quote\":\"As such, a distance within the Solar System without specifying the frame of reference for the measurement is problematic.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p15\",\"quote\":\"The astronomical unit is typically used for stellar system scale distances, such as the size of a protostellar disk or the heliocentric distance (distance from the Sun's centre) of an asteroid\"}]}"
